 qemu-system-x86_64 -> Runs the x86-64 system emulator.
   -machine q35 -> Uses the Intel Q35 chipset (modern PCIe-style layout).
                   Required for many modern PCI devices.EDU works on both pc and q35, but q35 is preferred
    -cpu qemu64 -> Uses QEMU’s generic 64-bit CPU model. Most stable choice for Yocto + emulation
    -smp 4 -> Creates 4 virtual CPUs.
    -m 256 -> Allocates 256 MB of RAM to the guest.
    -nographic -> Disables graphical output. Redirects VGA to serial
    -serial mon:stdio -> Multiplexes: QEMU monitor guest serial port (ttyS0)
    -device edu  -> PCI device
    -drive file=core-image-minimal-qemux86-64.rootfs-20260103203411.ext4,if=virtio,format=raw 
          -> if=virtio → fast paravirtualized disk
          -> format=raw → raw disk image Appears as /dev/vda
    -kernel bzImage
    -append "root=/dev/vda rw console=ttyS0 earlyprintk swiotlb=force"
          -> Kernel command line:
            root=/dev/vda → mount virtio disk as root
            rw → read/write root filesystem
            console=ttyS0 → primary console on serial
            earlyprintk → early boot debug output (very useful)

          -> Why swiotlb=force matters (EDU-specific)
             EDU uses 32-bit DMA
             Your guest RAM is above 4 GB physical addressing internally
             swiotlb=force ensures DMA buffers are reachable

